BLOG_POSTS["best-hotels-goa"] = {
  title: "Best Hotels in Goa 2025: Top Picks for Every Budget",
  category: "Travel",
  author: "WIBest Editorial",
  date: "2025-06-01",
  coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1512343879784-a960bf40e7f2?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
  summary: "Handpicked hotels in North and South Goa across luxury, mid-range, and budget tiers, with honest pros, cons, and price tips for 2025.",
  tags: ["Goa", "Hotels", "India Travel", "Beach Holidays"],
  content: `<h2>Choosing Where to Stay in Goa</h2><p>Goa splits cleanly into two moods. North Goa (Baga, Calangute, Anjuna, Vagator, Morjim) is lively, party-first, and cheaper. South Goa (Colva, Benaulim, Palolem, Agonda) is quieter, cleaner, and pricier per square foot of sand. Before booking, pick your vibe first, hotel second.</p><h2>Best Luxury Hotels (Rs 15,000+ per night)</h2><h3>Taj Exotica Resort & Spa, Benaulim</h3><p>56 acres on a quieter stretch of Benaulim beach. Villas from around Rs 28,000 off-season to Rs 55,000 in peak December. Best for honeymoons and families who want zero chaos.</p><h3>W Goa, Vagator</h3><p>Design-forward, loud, party-ready. Rooms from Rs 18,000. Woobar and the infinity pool are the draw. Not for light sleepers.</p><h3>Alila Diwa, Majorda</h3><p>Paddy-field views, South Goa calm, rooms from Rs 14,000. Quiet but still 15 minutes from Margao.</p><h2>Best Mid-Range (Rs 5,000 to 12,000)</h2><ul><li><strong>Novotel Goa Resort & Spa, Candolim</strong> — reliable, pool-forward, around Rs 8,500.</li><li><strong>The Postcard Velha, Panjim</strong> — heritage Portuguese villa, 12 rooms, Rs 9,500.</li><li><strong>Lazy Lagoon, Baga</strong> — Balinese-style, creek views, Rs 6,500 shoulder season.</li><li><strong>Stone Wood Resort, Morjim</strong> — boutique, walkable to beach, Rs 7,000.</li></ul><h2>Best Budget (Under Rs 4,000)</h2><ul><li><strong>Cuba Premium Suites, Patnem</strong> — Rs 3,200, spotless, pool.</li><li><strong>Zostel Goa, Anjuna</strong> — dorms from Rs 700, privates Rs 2,800.</li><li><strong>La Conceicao Boutique, Anjuna</strong> — Rs 3,500, run by a local family.</li></ul><h2>North vs South: Quick Table</h2><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>North Goa</th><th>South Goa</th></tr><tr><td>Vibe</td><td>Party, market, nightlife</td><td>Quiet, slow, beachy</td></tr><tr><td>Avg hotel price</td><td>Rs 4,000-10,000</td><td>Rs 7,000-15,000</td></tr><tr><td>Best beaches</td><td>Morjim, Ashwem, Vagator</td><td>Palolem, Agonda, Patnem</td></tr><tr><td>Getting around</td><td>Scooter Rs 400/day</td><td>Taxi or rented car better</td></tr></table><h2>Booking Tips for 2025</h2><p>Peak season (20 Dec to 5 Jan) prices triple. Book by September. For the best rate, compare Booking.com, MakeMyTrip, and the hotel's direct site; direct often includes breakfast free. Monsoon rates (Jun to Sep) can be 60% cheaper but many shacks close.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>For a first Goa trip with a partner, book Alila Diwa or Taj Exotica in the south. For a friends trip, W Goa or a villa in Anjuna. For a budget backpacker week, Zostel and scooter rentals keep you under Rs 15,000 all-in.</p>`,
  faq: [
    { q: "Is North or South Goa better for first-timers?", a: "North for nightlife and variety, South for relaxation. If unsure, split 3 nights north and 2 south." },
    { q: "When is the cheapest time to visit Goa?", a: "June to September (monsoon). Hotel rates drop 40-60% but some beach shacks and water sports shut." },
    { q: "Are Goa hotels safe for solo female travellers?", a: "Yes, especially branded properties in Panjim, Candolim, and Palolem. Avoid isolated shacks after dark." },
    { q: "Do Goa hotels charge extra for couples without marriage proof?", a: "Legally no, but some budget guesthouses ask for ID. Branded hotels do not." }
  ]
};

BLOG_POSTS["best-resorts-kerala"] = {
  title: "Best Resorts in Kerala 2025: Backwaters, Beaches, and Hills",
  category: "Travel",
  author: "WIBest Editorial",
  date: "2025-06-02",
  coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1602216056096-3b40cc0c9944?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
  summary: "The top resorts across Alleppey, Munnar, Kumarakom, Thekkady, and Varkala with honest pricing and what each is genuinely best for.",
  tags: ["Kerala", "Resorts", "Backwaters", "India Travel"],
  content: `<h2>How to Pick a Kerala Resort</h2><p>Kerala is not one destination. Alleppey and Kumarakom are backwaters. Munnar and Wayanad are hills and tea. Thekkady is wildlife. Varkala and Kovalam are beaches. Match the resort to the landscape, not the brand.</p><h2>Best Backwater Resorts</h2><h3>Kumarakom Lake Resort</h3><p>Heritage pool villas from Rs 22,000. Meandering pool that snakes past every villa. Ayurveda centre is genuinely good.</p><h3>Zuri Kumarakom</h3><p>Rs 12,000 range. Huge property, lakefront, strong breakfast. Best family pick.</p><h3>Xandari Riverscapes, Alleppey</h3><p>Private houseboat experience as floating villas. Rs 18,000 all-inclusive.</p><h2>Best Hill Resorts</h2><ul><li><strong>Windermere Estate, Munnar</strong> — Rs 11,000, guided tea walk.</li><li><strong>Spice Tree, Munnar</strong> — Rs 14,000, cliff-edge pool.</li><li><strong>Vythiri Village, Wayanad</strong> — Rs 9,500, rainforest treehouses.</li><li><strong>Spice Village, Thekkady</strong> — Rs 13,000, eco-stay near Periyar.</li></ul><h2>Best Beach Resorts</h2><ul><li><strong>Taj Green Cove, Kovalam</strong> — Rs 15,000, two coves.</li><li><strong>Niraamaya Retreats, Kovalam</strong> — Rs 20,000, best spa in Kerala.</li><li><strong>Hindustan Beach Retreat, Varkala</strong> — Rs 6,000, cliff views.</li></ul><h2>Quick Comparison</h2><table><tr><th>Region</th><th>Best For</th><th>Avg Price</th></tr><tr><td>Kumarakom</td><td>Backwater calm</td><td>Rs 12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Munnar</td><td>Honeymoon</td><td>Rs 11,000</td></tr><tr><td>Wayanad</td><td>Forest</td><td>Rs 9,000</td></tr><tr><td>Varkala</td><td>Budget beach</td><td>Rs 5,500</td></tr><tr><td>Kovalam</td><td>Luxury beach</td><td>Rs 16,000</td></tr></table><h2>Booking Tips</h2><p>October to March is peak. Monsoon is cheaper but backwater cruises limited. CGH Earth properties are eco-genuine. Book 45 days ahead for December rates.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>For a 7-day trip: 2 nights Munnar, 2 nights Alleppey houseboat + Kumarakom, 2 nights Varkala. Mid-range total for two, around Rs 75,000 with transfers.</p>`,
  faq: [
    { q: "Houseboat or lake resort?", a: "One night on a houseboat for novelty, then a lake resort for comfort and food quality." },
    { q: "Is Kerala worth visiting in monsoon?", a: "Yes for Ayurveda and prices, skip if you want beaches." },
    { q: "Munnar or Wayanad?", a: "Munnar for tea views, Wayanad for forests and wildlife." },
    { q: "Are Kerala resorts family-friendly?", a: "Yes. Zuri Kumarakom and Taj Green Cove are best set up for kids." }
  ]
};

BLOG_POSTS["best-places-to-visit-rajasthan"] = {
  title: "Best Places to Visit in Rajasthan 2025: A 10-Day Itinerary",
  category: "Travel",
  author: "WIBest Editorial",
  date: "2025-06-03",
  coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1477587458883-47145ed94245?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
  summary: "A practical ranked list of Rajasthan cities and towns with what to see, where to stay, and how many nights each deserves.",
  tags: ["Rajasthan", "India Travel", "Itinerary", "Heritage"],
  content: `<h2>The Rajasthan Big Five</h2><p>Everyone does Jaipur, Udaipur, Jodhpur, Jaisalmer, and Pushkar. We rank them by how much they reward time.</p><h3>1. Udaipur (3 nights)</h3><p>Most romantic city in India. City Palace, Lake Pichola boat ride at sunset, Bagore ki Haveli dance show (Rs 150). Stay: Taj Lake Palace (Rs 55,000) or Jagat Niwas (Rs 5,500).</p><h3>2. Jaisalmer (2 nights)</h3><p>Living fort, Sam sand dunes, camel safari. Stay inside the fort at Suryagarh (Rs 25,000) or Killa Bhawan (Rs 8,000). Desert camps Rs 3,000-12,000 per night.</p><h3>3. Jodhpur (2 nights)</h3><p>Mehrangarh Fort is arguably India's best fort. Clock Tower market, Jaswant Thada. Stay at RAAS (Rs 22,000) or Pal Haveli (Rs 6,500).</p><h3>4. Jaipur (2 nights)</h3><p>Amber Fort, City Palace, Hawa Mahal, block-print shopping in Bapu Bazaar. Stay at Rambagh Palace (Rs 40,000) or Pearl Palace (Rs 3,500).</p><h3>5. Pushkar (1 night)</h3><p>Lake, temple, hippie cafes. Enough for one night unless you hit Camel Fair (November).</p><h2>Under-Rated Picks</h2><ul><li><strong>Bundi</strong> — step-wells and a forgotten fort. 1 night.</li><li><strong>Shekhawati (Mandawa, Nawalgarh)</strong> — painted havelis. 2 nights.</li><li><strong>Ranthambore</strong> — tigers, book safari 90 days ahead.</li><li><strong>Mount Abu</strong> — hill retreat, Dilwara Temples.</li></ul><h2>10-Day Itinerary</h2><table><tr><th>Days</th><th>City</th></tr><tr><td>1-2</td><td>Jaipur</td></tr><tr><td>3-4</td><td>Jodhpur</td></tr><tr><td>5-6</td><td>Jaisalmer (incl. desert camp)</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Pushkar</td></tr><tr><td>8-10</td><td>Udaipur</td></tr></table><h2>Best Time and Budget</h2><p>October to March. Avoid May-June (45 C+). Budget for two, mid-range hotels, private AC car: Rs 1.2-1.5 lakh for 10 days. Backpacker version: Rs 35,000.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>Do Udaipur longer than you think, Jaipur shorter. Jaisalmer is the one people regret not adding. Skip Mount Abu on a first trip.</p>`,
  faq: [
    { q: "Is 7 days enough for Rajasthan?", a: "Yes for 3 cities: Jaipur, Jodhpur, Udaipur. For Jaisalmer too, allow 10." },
    { q: "Train or private car?", a: "Private car is 3x more comfortable. Trains work Jaipur-Jodhpur-Jaisalmer but not the full loop." },
    { q: "Is Rajasthan safe for solo female travellers?", a: "Generally yes in tourist areas. Stick to heritage hotels, avoid isolated late-night travel." },
    { q: "Best city for shopping?", a: "Jaipur for block prints and jewellery, Jodhpur for antiques, Udaipur for miniature paintings." }
  ]
};

BLOG_POSTS["best-hotels-jaipur"] = {
  title: "Best Hotels in Jaipur 2025: Palaces, Havelis, and Boutique Stays",
  category: "Travel",
  author: "WIBest Editorial",
  date: "2025-06-04",
  coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1599661046289-e31897846e41?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
  summary: "Top Jaipur hotels in every bracket, from Rambagh Palace to honest budget picks under Rs 3,000, with neighbourhood advice.",
  tags: ["Jaipur", "Hotels", "Rajasthan", "India Travel"],
  content: `<h2>Where to Stay in Jaipur</h2><p>Stay in Civil Lines or C-Scheme for central access, or near Amer if you want heritage and quiet. Avoid Sindhi Camp/railway area unless strictly budget.</p><h2>Luxury Palace Hotels</h2><h3>Rambagh Palace</h3><p>Taj's flagship. Former royal residence. Rooms from Rs 38,000, suites Rs 1 lakh+. Worth one splurge night even if you stay elsewhere.</p><h3>Raj Palace</h3><p>Heritage haveli, 250 years old. Rs 22,000. Smaller, more intimate than Rambagh.</p><h3>Oberoi Rajvilas</h3><p>Tented villas on 32 acres. Rs 45,000. Best spa in Jaipur.</p><h3>ITC Rajputana</h3><p>Rs 14,000. Central location, reliable for business travellers.</p><h2>Mid-Range (Rs 5,000-10,000)</h2><ul><li><strong>Samode Haveli</strong> — painted haveli, Rs 9,500, walking distance from Old City.</li><li><strong>Alsisar Haveli</strong> — heritage, Rs 7,500, pool.</li><li><strong>Trident Jaipur</strong> — Rs 8,000, lake views near Amer.</li><li><strong>Dera Rawatsar</strong> — Rs 6,500, boutique, excellent breakfast.</li></ul><h2>Budget (Under Rs 3,500)</h2><ul><li><strong>Pearl Palace</strong> — Rs 2,200, legendary traveller favourite.</li><li><strong>Zostel Jaipur</strong> — dorms Rs 650, privates Rs 2,400.</li><li><strong>Hotel Arya Niwas</strong> — Rs 3,000, old-school clean.</li></ul><h2>Comparison Table</h2><table><tr><th>Hotel</th><th>Area</th><th>Price</th><th>Best For</th></tr><tr><td>Rambagh Palace</td><td>Bhawani Singh Rd</td><td>Rs 38,000</td><td>Once-in-a-life</td></tr><tr><td>Samode Haveli</td><td>Gangapole</td><td>Rs 9,500</td><td>Heritage feel</td></tr><tr><td>Trident</td><td>Amer Rd</td><td>Rs 8,000</td><td>Families</td></tr><tr><td>Pearl Palace</td><td>Hari Kishan Somani Rd</td><td>Rs 2,200</td><td>Backpackers</td></tr></table><h2>When to Book</h2><p>Peak: October-March. Book 60 days early for December-January. Wedding-season surges are unpredictable, check direct with hotel.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>Best value pick: Samode Haveli. Best splurge: Rambagh. Best budget: Pearl Palace has been a traveller favourite for 20+ years for a reason.</p>`,
  faq: [
    { q: "Is Rambagh Palace worth Rs 38,000?", a: "For one night as an experience, yes. For a full stay, Samode Haveli gives 70% of the heritage feel at 25% of the price." },
    { q: "Which area is best for first-timers?", a: "C-Scheme or Civil Lines for central modern hotels; Gangapole for heritage walking-distance to the Pink City." },
    { q: "Are Jaipur heritage hotels authentic?", a: "Samode, Alsisar, and Raj Palace are genuinely old family properties. Many chain hotels are built in heritage style but are new." },
    { q: "Is Amer a good area to stay?", a: "Yes if you want quiet and lake views, but you will taxi everywhere. Good for 2-day stays, not 4-day." }
  ]
};

BLOG_POSTS["best-places-to-visit-kerala"] = {
  title: "Best Places to Visit in Kerala 2025: Ranked Guide",
  category: "Travel",
  author: "WIBest Editorial",
  date: "2025-06-05",
  coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1609952542675-1f3ae9b4f9d4?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
  summary: "Kerala's best destinations ranked for first-time and repeat visitors, with sample itineraries and honest time allocations.",
  tags: ["Kerala", "India Travel", "Itinerary", "Backwaters"],
  content: `<h2>The Classic Kerala Circuit</h2><p>Most first-timers do Kochi to Munnar to Thekkady to Alleppey to Varkala. It works, but it is fast. Here is what to prioritise.</p><h3>1. Alleppey and Kumarakom (2-3 nights)</h3><p>The backwaters are the one thing Kerala has that nothing else does. One night on a houseboat (Rs 12,000-25,000 all-inclusive) plus one night at a lake resort is the sweet spot.</p><h3>2. Munnar (2 nights)</h3><p>Tea gardens, Eravikulam National Park, Mattupetty Dam. Cool, misty, romantic. Skip if visiting in December if you hate crowds.</p><h3>3. Fort Kochi (2 nights)</h3><p>Chinese fishing nets, Jew Town, Paradesi Synagogue, Kathakali at Kerala Kathakali Centre (Rs 350). Walkable, cafes, best food scene in Kerala.</p><h3>4. Wayanad (2 nights)</h3><p>Edakkal Caves, Banasura Sagar Dam, Chembra Peak heart-shaped lake trek. Better than Munnar for families with teens.</p><h3>5. Varkala (2 nights)</h3><p>Cliff beach, cheaper than Kovalam, better cafes. North Cliff is backpacker zone, south is quieter.</p><h3>6. Thekkady / Periyar (1-2 nights)</h3><p>Bamboo rafting and boat safari on Periyar Lake. Wildlife sightings are hit-or-miss.</p><h2>Under-Rated</h2><ul><li><strong>Kannur</strong> — Theyyam ritual performances, uncrowded beaches.</li><li><strong>Bekal</strong> — fort and quiet beach.</li><li><strong>Athirapally Falls</strong> — Kerala's Niagara, good day trip from Kochi.</li></ul><h2>Sample 8-Day Itinerary</h2><table><tr><th>Day</th><th>Place</th></tr><tr><td>1-2</td><td>Fort Kochi</td></tr><tr><td>3-4</td><td>Munnar</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Thekkady</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Alleppey houseboat</td></tr><tr><td>7-8</td><td>Varkala</td></tr></table><h2>Budget</h2><p>For two people, mid-range, 8 days including car: Rs 85,000-1,10,000. Backpacker: Rs 28,000.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>If you only have 5 days: Kochi, Munnar, Alleppey. Skip Thekkady first time. Add Varkala if you have a week.</p>`,
  faq: [
    { q: "Best month for Kerala?", a: "October to February for weather, August-September for monsoon and Ayurveda." },
    { q: "Is Kerala good for solo female travel?", a: "Among the safest in India. Kochi, Varkala, and Munnar are all fine solo." },
    { q: "Kerala or Goa?", a: "Kerala for variety and nature, Goa for beach parties and easier logistics." },
    { q: "Do I need a car?", a: "A private car or driver for 8-10 days costs Rs 2,500-3,500/day and is worth it. Public transport works but eats time." }
  ]
};

BLOG_POSTS["best-hotels-manali"] = {
  title: "Best Hotels in Manali 2025: Old Manali to Solang",
  category: "Travel",
  author: "WIBest Editorial",
  date: "2025-06-06",
  coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1626621341517-bbf3d9990a23?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
  summary: "The best Manali hotels across areas with honest pricing, and why your choice of neighbourhood matters more than the hotel itself.",
  tags: ["Manali", "Himachal", "Hotels", "India Travel"],
  content: `<h2>Area First, Hotel Second</h2><p>Manali Mall Road is touristy and loud. Old Manali is cafes and backpackers. Hadimba/Log Huts is quiet with views. Naggar is 20 km away, peaceful heritage. Solang is ski-side, far from town.</p><h2>Luxury (Rs 12,000+)</h2><h3>Span Resort & Spa, Kullu</h3><p>Riverside, Rs 18,000, best spa in the valley. 40 min from Manali town but worth it.</p><h3>The Himalayan, Hadimba</h3><p>Castle-style building, Rs 15,000, atmospheric.</p><h3>Snow Valley Resorts</h3><p>Rs 12,000, near Mall Road, reliable.</p><h2>Mid-Range (Rs 4,000-8,000)</h2><ul><li><strong>Apple Country Resort</strong> — Rs 6,500, orchards, quiet.</li><li><strong>Larisa Resort</strong> — Rs 7,000, riverside.</li><li><strong>Manuallaya</strong> — Rs 5,500, spa focused.</li><li><strong>Solang Valley Resorts</strong> — Rs 6,000, near ski slopes.</li></ul><h2>Budget (Under Rs 2,500)</h2><ul><li><strong>Zostel Manali (Old Manali)</strong> — Rs 600 dorm, Rs 2,200 private.</li><li><strong>Drifters Inn</strong> — Rs 1,800, cafe culture.</li><li><strong>The Lazy Dog</strong> — adjoining the famous cafe, Rs 2,400.</li></ul><h2>Heritage in Naggar</h2><p>The Castle Naggar (HPTDC) is a 500-year-old fort hotel. Rs 4,000. 45 min from Manali but one of the best-kept secrets in Himachal.</p><h2>Comparison</h2><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Vibe</th><th>Best For</th></tr><tr><td>Mall Road</td><td>Touristy, loud</td><td>Families, short stays</td></tr><tr><td>Old Manali</td><td>Cafes, young crowd</td><td>Couples, backpackers</td></tr><tr><td>Hadimba</td><td>Forested, quiet</td><td>Honeymoon</td></tr><tr><td>Solang</td><td>Ski slopes</td><td>Adventure</td></tr><tr><td>Naggar</td><td>Heritage, rural</td><td>Repeat visitors</td></tr></table><h2>When to Book</h2><p>Peak: December-January (snow), May-June (summer escape). Prices double. Monsoon (July-August) risks landslides on the Chandigarh-Manali road; book flexible.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>First trip: Apple Country or Larisa. Couples: Himalayan or Manuallaya. Backpackers: Zostel Old Manali, no contest.</p>`,
  faq: [
    { q: "Is Old Manali or New Manali better?", a: "Old Manali for ambience and food, New Manali for convenience and shopping." },
    { q: "How many nights in Manali?", a: "3 nights for first-timers. Add 2 more if you plan Solang, Rohtang, and Naggar." },
    { q: "Is Manali safe in monsoon?", a: "The town is safe but the access road from Chandigarh has landslide risk. Fly to Bhuntar or Kullu if worried." },
    { q: "Hotel with best snow views?", a: "The Himalayan in Hadimba and Span Resort have the best winter views." }
  ]
};

BLOG_POSTS["best-places-to-visit-himachal"] = {
  title: "Best Places to Visit in Himachal Pradesh 2025",
  category: "Travel",
  author: "WIBest Editorial",
  date: "2025-06-07",
  coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1626621334053-7ca48c420e9b?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
  summary: "Himachal destinations ranked by what each does best, with honest takes on the overrated and genuinely magical spots.",
  tags: ["Himachal", "India Travel", "Mountains", "Itinerary"],
  content: `<h2>Himachal in One Line</h2><p>It is the most varied hill state in India. Manali is the gateway, but there are eight other places worth your week.</p><h3>1. Spiti Valley (5-7 days)</h3><p>Cold desert, ancient monasteries (Key, Tabo, Dhankar), Chandratal lake. Best June-September only. The most rewarding Himachal experience, and the most demanding.</p><h3>2. Kasol and Tosh (2-3 days)</h3><p>Parvati Valley, Israeli-cafe culture, riverside camps. Good first trek base.</p><h3>3. Dharamshala and McLeodganj (2-3 days)</h3><p>Dalai Lama's seat, monasteries, Triund trek (Rs 1,500-2,000 guided). Mix of spiritual and cafe.</p><h3>4. Manali (3-4 days)</h3><p>Covered separately. Gateway to Leh, Solang, Rohtang.</p><h3>5. Shimla (2 days)</h3><p>Colonial hill station. Mall Road, Jakhu Temple. Good base, not a destination.</p><h3>6. Kinnaur (4 days)</h3><p>Sangla, Chitkul (last Indian village before Tibet), apple orchards.</p><h3>7. Khajjiar and Dalhousie (2 days)</h3><p>Mini Switzerland. Quiet, good if Manali is too busy for you.</p><h3>8. Bir Billing (2 days)</h3><p>Paragliding capital of India. Rs 3,500 for 30-min flight.</p><h2>Suggested 10-Day Trip</h2><table><tr><th>Days</th><th>Place</th></tr><tr><td>1-2</td><td>Shimla</td></tr><tr><td>3-4</td><td>Kasol / Tosh</td></tr><tr><td>5-7</td><td>Manali + Solang</td></tr><tr><td>8-10</td><td>Dharamshala + Bir</td></tr></table><h2>Getting Around</h2><p>Private taxi Rs 3,000-4,000/day. HRTC Volvo buses are cheap and reliable. Trains only reach Kalka (toy train to Shimla) and Pathankot.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>Skip Shimla unless you are entering via Chandigarh. Spiti is the true hidden gem. Dharamshala is under-rated for repeat visitors.</p>`,
  faq: [
    { q: "Best time to visit Himachal?", a: "March-June and September-November. Winter (Dec-Feb) for snow but many high passes shut." },
    { q: "Is Spiti road safe?", a: "In June-September yes, with a good driver. Rest of the year passes are closed." },
    { q: "Manali or Dharamshala?", a: "Manali for activities and scale, Dharamshala for culture and quieter pace." },
    { q: "How many days for Himachal?", a: "Minimum 5 days for one region, 10-12 days for a two-region trip, 15+ for Spiti." }
  ]
};

